<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#berber-languages">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berber languages</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tamazight, Amazigh languages</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> languages, also called Tamazight, are a branch of the Afroasiatic family spoken by the Amazigh peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s Siwa Oasis and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> regions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a>. They have their own ancient script, Tifinagh. Tamazight became a national language of Algeria in 2002 and an official language of Morocco in 2011 and of Algeria in 2016.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berber-languages.jpg" alt="Berber languages" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ajellid-n-arif · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Berber_Languages_2018.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
